    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Lords exercised legal control over local markets and monopolized essential facilities (mills, ovens, bakehouses), imposing fixed fees (banalities) on peasants.",
      "evidence": "Manorial records and royal charters granting market rights show lords could set prices and require peasants to use lord‑owned facilities at set rates (e.g., the 13th‑century surveys of Kent and Sussex).",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Statutes such as the Statute of Labourers (1351) legally limited peasants’ wages and mobility, confirming that the system was designed to keep peasant labor cheap and under lordly control.",
      "evidence": "The text of the Statute of Labourers and analyses by historians (e.g., Hatcher, 1994) show it was enacted to curb rising wages after the Black Death, reinforcing lordly extraction.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The modern notion of “fair trade” requires voluntary, informed, and equal bargaining power between autonomous parties, a condition absent in feudal England.",
      "evidence": "Definitions from Fairtrade International and academic works on ethical trade stress free choice and equitable pricing; feudal serfs were legally bound to the land, could not freely choose trading partners, and faced monopolistic fees, making the required conditions impossible.",
